","We want to evaluate the definite integral
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} \left[ (x^2 + 3) - 1 \right] \, dx. $$
First, we simplify the expression in the integral:
$$ (x^2 + 3) - 1 = x^2 + 2. $$
So we have
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} (x^2 + 2) \, dx. $$
Now we find the antiderivative of $x^2 + 2$:
$$ \int (x^2 + 2) \, dx = \frac{x^3}{3} + 2x + C. $
We evaluate the integral at the bounds:
$$ \left[ \frac{x^3}{3} + 2x \right]_{-1}^{2} = \left( \frac{2^3}{3} + 2(2) \right) - \left( \frac{(-1)^3}{3} + 2(-1) \right) = \left( \frac{8}{3} + 4 \right) - \left( -\frac{1}{3} - 2 \right). $$
We have
$$ \frac{8}{3} + 4 = \frac{8}{3} + \frac{12}{3} = \frac{20}{3} $ and
$$ -\frac{1}{3} - 2 = -\frac{1}{3} - \frac{6}{3} = -\frac{7}{3}. $$
So the integral is
$$ \frac{20}{3} - \left( -\frac{7}{3} \right) = \frac{20}{3} + \frac{7}{3} = \frac{27}{3} = 9. $
Therefore,
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} (x^2 + 2) \, dx = 9. $
