We want to find $\lim_{t \to \infty} I(t) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + \lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} 4$.
Since $\lim_{t \to \infty} 4 = 4$, we need to find $\lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}$.
We have
$$ \left|\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right| \le \left|\frac{1}{3}\sin t\right| + |\cos t| \le \frac{1}{3} |\sin t| + |\cos t| \le \frac{1}{3} + 1 = \frac{4}{3}. $$
Thus,
$$ \left|\left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \frac{1}{3} \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}\right| \text{ and } \left|\left(\frac{1}{3} \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}\right| \text{ are not necessarily small}. $$
We can rewrite the expression as
$$ \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} = e^{-t/2} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right). $$
As $t \to \infty$, $e^{-t/2} \to 0$.
