","Let the surface be given by $F(x, y, z) = x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2 - 56 = 0$.
The vector $\mathbf{n}$ normal to the surface at a point $(x, y, z)$ is given by the gradient of $F$ at that point:
$$\mathbf{n} = \nabla F = \left( \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial F}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial}{ \partial z} \right) = (2x, 2y, 18z)$.
At the point $(4, 2, -2)$, the normal vector is
$$\mathbf{n} = (2(4), 2(2), 18(-2)) = (8, 4, -36)$.
The equation of the line normal to the surface at the point $(4, 2, -2)$ is given by
$$ \mathbf{r}(t) = \mathbf{p} + t \mathbf{n} = (4, 2, -2) + t (8, 4, -36) = (4+8t, 2 + 4t, -2 - 36t) $
The equation of the line can be expressed as
$$ x = 4 + 8t \\ y = 2 + 4t \\ z = -2 - 36t $
We can also express the equation of the line in the form
$$ \frac{x-4}{8} = \frac{y-2}{4} = \frac{z+2}{-36} $
