","Let the mass of the disk-like pulley be $m = 1$ kg and the radius of the disk be $R = 0.5$ m.
The tangential acceleration of point P on the rim of the pulley is $a_t = 2$ m/s$^2$.
The moment of inertia of a disk about its center is $I = \frac{1}{2}mR^2$.
The torque $\tau$ applied to the disk is given by $\tau = F \times R$, where $F$ is the force applied at point P.
The relationship between torque and angular acceleration is $\tau = I \alpha$, where $\alpha$ is the angular acceleration.
The tangential acceleration $a_t$ is related to the angular acceleration $\alpha$ by $a_t = R \alpha$.
We are given $a_t = 2$ m/s$^2$, so $\alpha = \frac{a_t}{R} = \frac{2}{0.5} = 4$ rad/s$^2$.
Now we can find the torque: $\tau = I \alpha = \frac{1}{2}mR^2 \times \frac{a_t}{R} = \frac{1}{2}m R a_t$.
Substituting the given values, we have $\tau = \frac{1}{2}(1 \text{ kg})(0.5 \text{ m})(2 \text{ m/s}^2) = 0.5 \text{ N m}$.
Since $\tau = F \times R$, we have $F = \frac{\tau}{R} = \frac{0.5 \text{ N m}}{0.5 \text{ m}} = 1 \text{ N}$.

Thus, the magnitude of the force F is 1 N.

